---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import {
  image,
  versionedImage,
  dockerHub,
  docHref,
  repository,
} from '../../lib/site';
---

<Doc
  title="Run Curlimp with Docker"
  slug="docker"
  section="Getting started"
  description="Use the published curlimp Docker image, mount host files, pass environment variables and understand container boundaries."
>
  <h2>Use the ready-to-run image</h2>
  <p>
    The public <a href={dockerHub}>Docker Hub image</a> already includes the
    CLI, native engine, libraries and CA certificates. Its entrypoint is the
    HTTP client.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm ${image} -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    Options before the image name belong to Docker. Options after it belong to
    curlimp. You do not need a separate pull when the image is not yet present
    locally.
  </p>
  <h2>Choose a browser profile</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm ${image} firefox147 -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    Chrome 150 and response decompression are enabled by default. For the native
    <code>curlimp</code> command and Zsh profile autocomplete, follow the{' '}
    <a href={docHref('installation')}>installation guide</a> and{' '}
    <a href={docHref('completion')}>completion setup</a>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="updates-and-fixed-versions">Updates and fixed versions</h2>
  <p>
    For everyday use, the examples use <code>{image}</code> without a tag.
    Docker defaults to <code>latest</code>, which follows the newest stable
    release after both Linux architectures pass validation. Release candidates
    never replace it. Versioned tags stay tied to their release.
  </p>
  <p>
    Omitting the tag does not mean that every invocation downloads an update.
    Docker reuses a local image by default. Refresh the image used by your
    command with:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`docker pull ${image}`} />
  <p>
    To check the registry before every invocation, add Docker's{' '}
    <code>--pull=always</code> before the image name. Each invocation then needs
    registry access:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm --pull=always ${image} \\\n  -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    For scripts or CI, choose a fixed version so that updates are deliberate:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm ${versionedImage} \\\n  -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    Use an image digest when immutable selection is required. The repository's
    root Dockerfile derives from the published release runtime. Override
    <code>RUNTIME_IMAGE</code> to use another version or a local build. Review
    the <a href={`${repository}/releases`}>release notes</a> when upgrading;
    default profiles and supported options can change between releases.
  </p>
  <h2>Mount host files</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/work" ${image} \\\n  -sS -D /work/response.headers -o /work/response.body \\\n  https://httpbin.org/status/418`}
  />
  <p>
    A CLI path inside Docker points into the container. Shell redirection, such
    as <code>&gt; response.body</code>, is handled by your host shell. See{' '}
    <a href={docHref('files')}>files and pipelines</a> for uploads and stdin.
  </p>
  <h2>Pass a default profile through the environment</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`docker run --rm -e CURL_IMPERSONATE=firefox147 \\\n  ${image} -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    Setting an environment variable in your host shell does not automatically
    pass it into a container. Use Docker's <code>-e</code>, or choose the
    profile with a CLI flag.
  </p>
  <h2>Know the runtime</h2>
  <p>
    The production image packages the glibc runtime into a minimal filesystem.
    It has no interactive shell or package manager. Docker is running the Linux
    build even on macOS or Windows. Both Linux amd64 and arm64 are published
    under the same tag.
  </p>
  <p>
    The root Dockerfile derives from a published runtime image. The separate
    maintainer Dockerfile compiles the engine and CLI; see{' '}
    <a href={docHref('building')}>building from source</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
